# Normalise on commit. Everything in this tree is source or a config we ship,
# so a CRLF in the index is always wrong. ("auto" still leaves real binaries
# alone, should any turn up.)
* text=auto

# Force LF in the working tree as well, for everything that ends up inside the
# ISO. This matters more than it looks: a CR is invisible in a diff, and it
# breaks things silently. `/etc/profile` with CRLF puts a CR at the end of
# $PATH and every command comes up "not found"; `/etc/passwd` and `/etc/hosts`
# fail their lookups; and the scripts we hand to busybox ash turn each line
# into "command not found" at boot — on a machine with no shell to log into
# and fix it with.
#
# It must stay `text=auto`. A bare `text` here says "this file is text even if
# it is obviously not", and git then strips every CRLF byte pair out of the
# blob on commit. That is invisible for source but fatal for an archive: it is
# how iso/plymouth/plymouth-frames.zip was destroyed — the committed blob has
# zero CRLF pairs (a 28 MB binary should have hundreds), so every member's
# deflate stream past the first two fails its CRC and the zip cannot even
# list. `auto` runs the same binary detection git uses everywhere else and
# leaves such files byte-for-byte alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The scripts busybox ash runs don't end in .sh at all: the initramfs init has
# no extension, and the DHCP lease script is *.script.
init text eol=lf
*.script text eol=lf

# The Plymouth frames (iso/plymouth/copper/frames) are PNG. `text=auto` above
# already detects them as binary and leaves them alone, but saying so explicitly
# skips that detection for all 150 of them on every commit — and makes the
# intent local to the one directory where a mis-detected binary would cost the
# whole boot animation.
*.png binary

# Unit files are config, and config is text: they must reach the image with LF
# line endings, which `eol=lf` above already gives them. What git cannot carry
# is the MODE, and that matters here: unit files are 0644 root:root in the
# image, and on a Windows checkout git writes the working tree 777 (as it does
# for every file in this repo). systemd refuses to *read* a world-writable unit
# without a warning and refuses to run one whose ExecStart is executable-but-
# not-owned, so the mode is pinned twice: build_systemd chmods what it installs,
# and this comment exists so nobody "fixes" that chmod away as redundant.
# (It is not: see the gate in build_systemd, which exists because a unit that
# installed 0777 looked exactly like a unit that installed correctly.)
*.service text eol=lf
*.target text eol=lf
*.link text eol=lf
*.network text eol=lf

# The console font in iso/rootfs-overlay/usr/share/consolefonts is a PSF1
# bitmap, the one binary that has to reach the kernel byte-for-byte: its glyph
# data is the difference between fastfetch's box drawing and a screen of "?".
# `text=auto` already sees the NUL in the header and leaves it alone, but an
# EOL rule applied to a bitmap is the same class of bug as the destroyed
# plymouth zip, so -- like *.png above -- the intent is written down here and
# the cost is one line of git config.
*.psf binary
